// Three trees, and the whole of what makes them is one primitive that calls itself.
//
// `limb` is thirty lines.  It makes one length of wood and then asks for three more limbs at the top
// of it, each shorter, thinner and turned away from the last.  Those ask for three more, and so on --
// until `depth` runs out, at which point it stops asking and puts out leaves instead.
//
// **That stopping is the whole reason this scene exists.**  A thing that calls itself needs somewhere
// to stop, and until a body could choose between two answers there was no way to write one.  With a
// choice there is: the first arm is the end of a limb and the second is a limb that carries three
// more.  Everything else here follows from that one sentence.  Take the choice away and this is not a
// scene that gets harder to write -- it is a scene that cannot be written at all, at any length,
// because the recursion has no floor.
//
// Count what is standing here.  The two nearer trees are four generations of three, which is forty
// limbs and eighty-one clusters of leaves apiece; with the smaller one behind them that is some six
// hundred and sixty pieces of geometry, and not one of them is mentioned by name anywhere in this
// file.  Written out by hand it would be an unreadable thousand lines, and no one would ever alter it,
// because altering how a tree branches would mean editing every branch.
//
// Here it means editing one number.  `shrink` decides how much shorter each generation is; `lean` how
// far a limb turns away from its parent; `taper` how much thinner it gets.  Change `lean` from 34 to
// 20 and all three trees become narrow and upright together.  Change `shrink` and they become
// top-heavy or spindly.  The trees were never described -- a rule for growing one was, and the rule
// was applied.
//
// **No two limbs are alike, and that is what makes them read as trees.**  A rule applied faithfully
// gives a diagram: every fork the same angle, every limb the same fraction of its parent, the whole
// thing as regular as a snowflake and about as convincing.  So every limb departs a little from the
// rule -- a bit longer or shorter, leaning a bit more or less, turned a bit further round -- and the
// departure comes from `random`, which hands back a number between 0 and 1 for whatever key it is
// given.  `vary` says how far a limb may stray; set it to 0 and the diagram comes back.
//
// The key is what makes this reproducible rather than merely irregular.  Every limb carries a number
// of its own -- `id`, which is its parent's number times three plus which child it is, so no two limbs
// anywhere in the file share one -- and asks for its wobbles by that number.  So the same file always
// grows the same three trees, down to the last leaf; adding a fourth tree cannot disturb the first
// three; and were these to be animated, nothing would crawl.  Change `variant` at the top and the
// same three trees grow again, differently, which is the one knob a person actually wants.
//
// The three trees themselves differ in what they were told: how deep to go, how long the first limb
// is, which way the first fork faces, and which number they start from.
//
// Two smaller points worth noticing, since both are what make the recursion read as plainly as it
// does:
//
//   The body uses `shrink`, `taper` and `lean` without being handed them.  A body sees the names of
//   the file it was **written** in, so the three numbers that decide the whole habit of the tree sit
//   at the top of the file where a reader will find them, rather than being threaded through every
//   recursive call as three more values.
//
//   Only the arm taken is carried out.  The leaf arm never asks for more limbs, so the recursion ends
//   where it is told to and not one call later.
//
//   `wilt` and `sway` are ordinary functions of the scene's own.  A limb needs several wobbles that
//   have nothing to do with each other, so each asks `random` with the limb's number *and* a second
//   key saying which wobble is wanted -- which is what a second key is for.
//
// The light is the physical sky, low and to the side, which is what puts the long shadows across the
// grass and lets the crowns be lit from above and filled from the blue overhead.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'Recursive Trees'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'Some six hundred pieces of geometry from one primitive that calls itself, no two alike.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

// The habit of the tree, said once.  Every limb of every tree reads these, and nothing else decides
// how any of them grows.
shrink = 0.74      // how much shorter each generation is than the one below it
taper = 0.62       // and how much thinner
lean = 34          // how far a limb turns away from the one it grew from
turn = 118         // and how far round, from one of its siblings to the next
vary = 0.28        // and how far any one limb is allowed to depart from all of that
variant = 1        // change this and the same three trees grow again, differently

duller = color [0.23, 0.29, 0.14]
greener = color [0.31, 0.37, 0.19]

bark = material {
    pigment [0.52, 0.36, 0.24]
    specular 0.12
    shininess 20
}

leaves = material {
    pigment [0.29, 0.47, 0.20]
    specular 0.18
    shininess 30
}

// Two small functions that turn a limb's number into a wobble.  `wilt` gives something near one, so a
// limb comes out a little shorter or a little longer than the rule says; `sway` gives something near
// zero, so it turns a little further round or a little less.  Both take the limb's own number and a
// second key saying which wobble is wanted, since one limb needs several that have nothing to do with
// each other.
function wilt(id, which) -> number { return 1 - vary + random(id, which, variant) * vary * 2 }
function sway(id, which) -> number { return (random(id, which, variant) - 0.5) * lean }

// One limb: a length of wood, and then either leaves or three more limbs.
//
// The choice is the base case.  With `depth` spent, the limb ends in a cluster of leaves and asks for
// nothing further; while it has depth left, it carries three more limbs, each one generation smaller.
primitive limb(depth, length, thickness, twist, id) -> group {
    if (depth < 1) {
        return group {
            sphere { material leaves  scale [0.20, 0.13, 0.20] }
            sphere { material leaves  scale [0.15, 0.10, 0.15]  translate [0.15, 0.07, 0.09] }
            sphere { material leaves  scale [0.13, 0.09, 0.13]  translate [-0.12, 0.05, -0.11] }
        }
    }
    else {
        return group {
            // A tube rather than a cylinder, because a cylinder ends in a flat disc: the next limb
            // leaves that disc at an angle and the disc is left standing proud, edge-on, so every
            // fork gets a dark band across it and the tree reads as pipes stacked end to end.  A tube
            // ends in a hemisphere, which looks the same from every direction, and it tapers along
            // its length as well, so a limb no longer steps down where it meets the next.
            tube {
                material bark
                radius thickness at [0, 0, 0]
                radius thickness * taper at [0, length, 0]
            }
            object limb(depth - 1, length * wilt(id, 1), thickness * taper, twist + turn, id * 3 + 1) {
                rotate Z lean * wilt(id, 2)
                rotate Y twist + sway(id, 3)
                translate Y length
            }
            object limb(depth - 1, length * wilt(id, 4), thickness * taper, twist + turn, id * 3 + 2) {
                rotate Z lean * wilt(id, 5)
                rotate Y twist + turn + sway(id, 6)
                translate Y length
            }
            // The third leans less, so a crown does not come out as a flat fan.
            object limb(depth - 1, length * wilt(id, 7) * 0.88, thickness * taper, twist + turn, id * 3 + 3) {
                rotate Z lean * 0.45 * wilt(id, 8)
                rotate Y twist + turn * 2 + sway(id, 9)
                translate Y length * 0.94
            }
        }
    }
}

camera {
    location [6.6, 2.7, -13.0]
    look at [-0.4, 3.6, 0.4]
    field of view 50
}

background physical sky {
    sun elevation 27
    sun azimuth 142
    turbidity 2.6
    brightness 3
}

sky light {
    samples 28
}

// The ground, mottled a little so that a flat sheet of green does not give the trees away.
plane {
    material {
        pigment bozo {
            turbulence 0.4
            [0, duller, 1, greener]
            scale 1.8
        }
        specular 0.03
    }
}

// Three trees, differing only in what they were told.
object limb(4, 1.9, 0.17, 0, 1) { }
object limb(4, 1.5, 0.14, 55, 2) { translate [-4.2, 0, 2.2] }
object limb(3, 1.3, 0.11, 200, 3) { translate [3.2, 0, 5.4] }
